confidence: high
callouts:
- type: definition
  title: Peak End Rule
  body: The Peak End Rule is the empirical finding, established by Kahneman, Fredrickson,
    and colleagues, that retrospective evaluations of an extended hedonic experience
    are well predicted by the average of its most intense moment and its final moment,
    with surprisingly little weight given to the experience's overall duration.
- type: key-claim
  title: Key claim about Peak End Rule
  body: The Peak End Rule produces the counterintuitive prediction — confirmed in
    the cold-pressor and colonoscopy studies — that adding a period of mild discomfort
    to the end of an unpleasant experience can improve its remembered evaluation,
    because the milder ending lowers the "end" anchor while the peak is unchanged
    and duration is largely ignored.
- type: warning
  title: Common pitfall regarding Peak End Rule
  body: The Peak End Rule applies to retrospective summaries used in future decisions,
    not to the moment-by-moment experience itself; designing interventions around
    the Peak End Rule risks improving memories at the cost of total experienced wellbeing,
    raising a serious normative question about whether the experiencing self or the
    remembering self should govern the design of services and treatments.
